# Taskmark project memory

If a Taskmark board exists in the workspace, treat it as the read-only reference for planned, in-flight, and done product work.

## Reconciliation belongs to the App

- Agents read and plan. The request brief, the item description, and its
  acceptance or fix criteria guide the implementation.
- The Taskmark GitHub / Bitbucket App reconciles on each push and merged pull
  request: done status, stages, dates, resolvers, checked criteria, Work log,
  and Commits.
- Humans accept the App's proposals in the review inbox, and shelve or cancel
  items by hand when work is dropped.
- Never change an existing epic, story, task, bug, request brief, or meeting
  file in any way: no status, dates, resolvers, checked criteria, Prompt &
  feedback, Work log, or Commits rows, and no text edits.

## Rules

1. Board location:
   - One git product: `<project>/taskmark/`.
   - Multiple git products: dedicated sibling `<common>-taskmark` repository
     root. Never copy the board into product repos.
   - `REPOS.md` is local-generated and gitignored.
   - `npx taskmark init` sets the board up and migrates legacy boards.
   - Layout: a 2.0 request board keeps `requests/<slug>/brief.md`,
     `requests/<slug>/epics/...`, `requests/<slug>/log/`, and `meetings/`; a
     legacy board keeps `epics/`. Request logs, meetings, and task stages are
     written by the App and people, never by agents.
2. Before product work, search for a matching item (and, on a request board,
   its request brief) and use it as the spec, whether it is open or already
   done. Use `/tkmd-plan`, `/tkmd-save`,
   `/tkmd-save-do`, or `/tkmd-plan-do` only when nothing fits.
3. Use only `/tkmd-init`, `/tkmd-verify`, `/tkmd-plan`, `/tkmd-save`,
   `/tkmd-save-do`, `/tkmd-plan-do`, `/tkmd-do`, `/tkmd-changelog`,
   `/tkmd-version`, `/tkmd-reportme`, and `/tkmd-commit`.
4. Creation writes new item files only: under an existing request or a new
   request brief on a request board, under `epics/` on a legacy board. Never
   edit existing parent markdown or an existing brief to add links, rollups,
   logs, people, status, or dates.
5. `/tkmd-do` implements the target, never commits or pushes, never uses an
   `in_progress` stage, and never touches board files. Before changing code it
   works on a branch whose name contains the item id (`<id>-<short-slug>`).
   Commits keep the id in the branch or message; the `prepare-commit-msg` hook
   from `npx taskmark hooks install` adds a `Task: <id>` trailer.
6. `/tkmd-commit` is the only user command that commits.
7. `/tkmd-changelog` writes only board-root `CHANGELOG.md`. `/tkmd-version`
   writes that changelog plus the chosen SemVer on the board `package.json`,
   every linked product-root `package.json`, and the Cursor plugin
   `plugin.json`. They never commit or write a README changelog.
   `/tkmd-reportme` writes only `.reports/report-YYYYMMDD.md` on the board,
   reporting the current git identity's done leaves since the previous report.
   That directory is local-generated and gitignored.
8. Parent hierarchy, rollups, logs, status, implementers, and lifecycle dates
   are read-time views over descendant leaves.
9. Never create or refresh `INDEX.md`, `SIZING.md`, `VELOCITY.md`, or a
   generated board dashboard README. `/tkmd-init` may create or repair the
   static project README (product-root, or `*-taskmark` board root).
   `/tkmd-verify` is the only exception for existing items: a maintainer-only
   migration that deletes leftover generated files and strips retired
   frontmatter and parent child lists, never status, logs, commits, or
   criteria. Never write a Last synced field or a README changelog.
10. Agent-written board markdown uses the board writing language stored as
    `taskmark.writingLanguage` in board `package.json` (any language chosen at
    init; default is the usual Cursor-agent language). Chat language does not
    change that. If the field is missing, use the init default. Do not
    translate existing files unless the current task explicitly rewrites them.
